prompts.librarian/merge:
Merge an incoming note into an existing wiki note and return the complete updated note.

{{shared/note_rules}}

How to merge:
- The result is the existing note, updated: keep its title, summary, headings and order of sections, and its wording where nothing changed. Do not rewrite or reorganise it around the incoming note.
- Keep every fact of the existing note that the incoming note does not contradict.
- Put each new fact in the section where it belongs; add a section only when none fits.
- When the incoming note updates or contradicts the existing one, keep the newer fact and state the change in place, with the date of the incoming note ("As of March 2026 the limit is 40, previously 30"), unless the incoming note is clearly older than the existing one.
- Change the title and summary only if the subject itself changed, not because the incoming note is longer or has another focus.

Existing note
Title: {{title}}
Summary: {{summary}}
Tags: {{tags}}
<existing>
{{body}}
</existing>

Incoming note ({{incoming_from}})
Title: {{incoming_title}}
Summary: {{incoming_summary}}
Tags: {{incoming_tags}}
<note>
{{incoming_body}}
</note>

Reply with JSON:
{"title": "...", "summary": "...", "tags": ["..."], "body": "..."}

prompts.librarian/merge_source:
Merge a new source into an existing wiki note and return the complete updated note.

{{shared/note_rules}}

How to merge:
- Keep every fact of the existing note that the source does not contradict.
- Put each new fact in the section where it belongs; add a section only when none fits.
- When the source updates or contradicts the note, keep the newer fact and state the change in place ("As of March 2026 the limit is 40, previously 30"), unless the source is clearly older than the note.
- Never shorten the note to the size of the source and never drop sections.
- Change the title and summary only if the scope of the subject changed.

Existing note
Title: {{title}}
Summary: {{summary}}
Tags: {{tags}}
<existing>
{{body}}
</existing>

New source
<source>
{{source_text}}
</source>

Reply with JSON:
{"title": "...", "summary": "...", "tags": ["..."], "body": "..."}

prompts.librarian/name_folder:
Name the new folder for the incoming note.

Use:
- one to three lowercase words;
- hyphens between words;
- letters a-z and digits only;
- a broad category, not the specific article title.

The description must be one short line, at most 120 characters.
Do not explain your choice.

Parent folder: {{parent}}
Parent description: {{parent_description}}

Existing subfolders:
{{siblings}}

<note>
{{note}}
</note>

Return exactly:
{
  "name": "...",
  "description": "..."
}

prompts.librarian/plan:
An empty knowledge base is about to receive these documents (title: opening):
{{documents}}

Lay out its folders before the first one is filed, so that every document finds its place.

{{shared/folder_rules}}

- At most {{top}} top-level folders: the broad areas these documents belong to. A single client, supplier, project or product is never a top-level folder, however many documents are about it: it is a subfolder of the broad area it belongs to.
- A subfolder only for a specific subject that at least 2 of these documents share (one client, one supplier, a project, a product line, a recurring kind of document such as weekly meetings); at most {{depth}} levels in all.
- A folder that has subfolders has at least two, and together they hold all of its documents; a subject with no second subject beside it stays in its parent.
- Names: 1 to 3 lowercase words joined by hyphens, letters a-z and digits only, in the language of the documents; a plural noun for collections.

Reply with JSON, parents before their subfolders:
{"folders": [{"path": "/area", "description": "..."}, {"path": "/area/subject", "description": "..."}]}

prompts.librarian/plan_more:
A knowledge base already has these folders (path: description; notes directly in it):
{{tree}}

These documents are about to be filed (title: opening):
{{documents}}

Lay out only the NEW folders these documents need: {{roots}}
subfolder only for a specific subject (one client, one supplier, a project, a product line, a recurring kind of document)
that at least 2 of these documents share and no existing folder covers. Never repeat, rename or move an existing folder.
At most {{depth}} levels in all. Names: 1 to 3 lowercase words joined by hyphens, letters a-z and digits.
It is fine to answer with no folders.

Reply with JSON, parents before their subfolders:
{"folders": [{"path": "/area/subject", "description": "..."}]}

prompts.librarian/relate:
Select only direct related notes.

Link notes that describe:
- the same entity from another angle;
- a direct cause or consequence;
- a prerequisite;
- a direct alternative.

Do not link notes that only share a broad topic.
Return at most {{max_links}} exact paths.
Return an empty list when none qualifies.

<note>
{{note}}
</note>

Candidates:
<notes>
{{notes}}
</notes>

Return exactly:
{
  "reasoning": "one short sentence",
  "related": ["<exact path>", "..."]
}

prompts.librarian/route:
Choose where the note belongs.

Use "descend" when an existing subfolder clearly covers the subject.
Use "here" when the current folder is correct.
Use "new" only when a durable new category is needed.

Do not invent folder names unless action is "new".
Do not explain your choice.

Current folder: {{folder}}
Description: {{description}}

Subfolders:
{{subfolders}}

<note>
{{note}}
</note>

Return exactly:
{
  "reasoning": "one short sentence",
  "action": "descend" | "here" | "new",
  "subfolder": "name" | null
}

prompts.librarian/route_fallback:
The automatic router was not confident about where the incoming note belongs. These are the folders it considered (path: description):
{{folders}}

<note>
{{note}}
</note>

{{shared/folder_rules}}

Choose one action:
- "select": file the note directly in one of the folders above; put its path (for example "/finance/pricing") in "folder". Never "/": the root holds no notes.
- "create": the note needs a new subfolder; put in "folder" the path of the listed folder under which it must be created ("/" for a new top-level folder). It is named in a later step.

Reply with JSON:
{"reasoning": "...", "action": "select" | "create", "folder": "/path"}

prompts.librarian/split:
A folder of a knowledge base holds these notes directly (number. title: summary):
{{notes}}

The folder: {{folder}}: {{description}}
Its subfolders now (path: description):
{{subfolders}}

Open subfolders of {{folder}} for the specific subjects (one client, one supplier, a project, a product line, a person or
team, a recurring kind of document) that at least 2 of these notes share and no subfolder above covers. For each, list
the numbers of its notes: they are moved there. Notes that share their subject with no other listed note stay where they
are, and so does what is about the folder's subject as a whole. Never a subfolder for whatever is left over. Each note in
at most one subfolder. Never rename or move {{folder}}. Names: 1 to 3 lowercase words joined by hyphens, letters a-z and
digits; a description says what belongs there. It is fine to open none.

Reply with JSON: {"folders": [{"name": "...", "description": "...", "notes": [1, 2]}]}

